Wendy Hall was born in west London in 1952. She studied mathematics at the University of Southampton, earning her PhD in 1977, and later took a master's degree in computing.

## Hypertext before the web

In 1984 she returned to Southampton to join its new computer science group, working on multimedia and hypermedia. Her team invented the Microcosm hypermedia system before the World Wide Web existed. Microcosm stored links separately from the documents they pointed at, so links could be added to any file and kept up to date in one place. It was later turned into a company, Multicosm Ltd.

## Building Web Science

- **1994:** Became Southampton's first female professor of engineering.
- **2006:** Co-founded the Web Science Research Initiative with Tim Berners-Lee, Nigel Shadbolt and Daniel Weitzner. It helped establish Web Science, the study of how the web works and how it shapes society.
- **2008–2010:** Served as President of the Association for Computing Machinery (ACM), the first person from outside North America to hold the role.
- **2009:** Made a Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire.
- **2017:** Appointed Regius Professor of Computer Science at Southampton.

She has also chaired the Ada Lovelace Institute and advised the UK government on artificial intelligence.

